.TH INTRO 2
.SH NAME
intro, errno \- introduction to system calls and error numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "#include <errno.h>"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Section 2 of this manual lists all the entries into the system.
Use of
many of these calls is discussed in the chapter ``VENIX Programming''
in the
.IR "Programming Guide" .
You might also consider use of the standard
I/O library, described in section 3 of this manual as well as in ``VENIX
Programming,'' for easier use of many of the I/O functions.
Be careful
not to mix system-level I/O calls (such as
.B open()
and
.BR close() )
with standard
I/O calls (such as
\fBfopen()\fRand
.BR fclose() ).
.PP
Most of these calls have an error return.
An error condition is indicated
by an otherwise impossible returned value.
Almost always this is \-1; the
individual sections specify the details.
An error number is also made
available in the external variable
.BR errno .
.B errno
is not cleared on successful
calls, so it should be tested only after an error has occurred.
.PP
There is a table of messages associated with each error, and a routine for
printing the message; see
.BR perror (3).
The possible error numbers are not
recited with each writeup in section 2, since many errors are possible for
most of the calls.
Here is a list of the error numbers, their names as
defined in
.BR <errno.h> ,
and the messages available using
.BR perror .
.PP
Errors which have an `*' preceding the number may have an accompanying
system error message on the main console device, since they represent
conditions which the system administrator should be aware of.
See
``VENIX Maintenance'' in the
.I "Installation and System Manager's Guide"
for a listing of system error messages.
.TP 5
0
\- Error 0 Unused
.TP
1
\fBEPERM\fR \- Not owner
.br
Typically this error indicates an attempt to modify a file in some
way forbidden except to its owner or super-user.
It is also
returned for attempts by ordinary users to do things allowed only
to the super-user.
.TP
2
\fBENOENT\fR \- No such file or directory
.br
This error occurs when a file name is specified and the file should
exist but doesn't, or when one of the directories in a pathname
does not exist.
.TP
3
\fBESRCH\fR \- No such process
.br
The process whose number was given to
.BR signal ,
.BR suspend ,
or
.B ptrace
does not exist, or is already dead.
.TP
4
\fBEINTR\fR \- Interrupted system call
.br
An asynchronous signal (such as interrupt or quit), which the
user has elected to catch, occurred during a system call.
If execution
is resumed after processing the signal, it will appear as if
the interrupted system call returned this error condition.
.TP
*5
\fBEIO\fR \- I/O error
.br
Some physical I/O error occurred during a
.B read
or
.BR write .
This
error may in some cases occur on a call following the one to
which it actually applies.
.TP
6
\fBENXIO\fR \- No such device or address
.br
I/O on a special file refers to a subdevice that does not exist, or
beyond the limits of the device.
It may also occur when, for
example, a tape drive is not dialed in or no disk pack is loaded
on a drive.
.TP
7
\fBE2BIG\fR \- Arg list too long
.br
An argument list longer than 2048 bytes is presented to
.BR exec .
.TP
8
\fBENOEXEC\fR \- Exec format error
.br
A request is made to execute a file which, although it has the
appropriate permissions, does not start with a valid magic
number, see
.BR a.out (4).
.TP
9
\fBEBADF\fR \- Bad file number
.br
Either a file descriptor refers to no open file, or a read (resp.
write) request is made to a file that is open only for writing (resp.
reading).
.TP
10
\fBECHILD\fR \- No children
.br
.B wait
and the process has no living or unwaited-for children.
.TP
11
\fBEAGAIN\fR \- No more processes
.br
In a
.BR fork ,
the system's process table is full or the user is not
allowed to create any more processes.
.TP
*12
\fBENOMEM\fR \- Not enough core
.br
During an
.B exec
or
.BR break ,
a program asks for more core than the
system is able to supply.
This is not a temporary condition; the
maximum core size is a system parameter.
The error may also
occur if the arrangement of text, data, and stack segments
requires too many segmentation registers, or if there is not
enough swap space available.
.TP
13
\fBEACCES\fR \- Permission denied
.br
An attempt was made to access a file in a way forbidden by the
protection system.
.TP
14
\fBEFAULT\fR \- Bad address
.br
The system encountered a hardware fault in attempting to access
the arguments of a system call.
.TP
15
\fBENOTBLK\fR \- Block device required
.br
A plain file was mentioned where a block device was required,
e.g. in
.BR mount .
.TP
16
\fBEBUSY\fR \- Mount device busy
.br
An attempt to mount a device that was already mounted or an
attempt was made to dismount a device on which there is an
active file (open file, current directory, mounted-on file, active
text segment).
.TP
17
\fBEEXIST\fR \- File exists
.br
An existing file was mentioned in an inappropriate context, e.g.
.BR link .
.TP
18
\fBEXDEV\fR \- Cross-device link
.br
A link to a file on another device was attempted.
.TP
19
\fBENODEV\fR \- No such device
.br
An attempt was made to apply an inappropriate system call to a
device, e.g. read a write-only device.
.TP
20
\fBENOTDIR\fR \- Not a directory
.br
A non-directory was specified where a directory is required, for
example in a pathname or as an argument to
.BR chdir .
.TP
21
\fBEISDIR\fR \- Is a directory
.br
An attempt to write on a directory.
.TP
22
\fBEINVAL\fR \- Invalid argument
.br
Some invalid argument: dismounting a non-mounted device,
mentioning an unknown signal in
.BR signal ,
reading or writing a file
for which
.B seek
has generated a negative pointer.
Also set by
math functions, see
.BR intro (3).
.TP
*23
\fBENFILE\fR \- File table overflow
.br
The system's table of open files is full, and temporarily no more
.B opens
can be accepted.
.TP
24
\fBEMFILE\fR \- Too many open files
.br
Customary configuration limit is 15 per process.
.TP
25
\fBENOTTY\fR \- Not a typewriter
.br
The file mentioned in
.B ioctl
is not a terminal or one of the other
devices to which these calls apply.
.TP
26
\fBETXTBSY\fR \- Text file busy
.br
An attempt to execute a pure-procedure program that is currently
open for writing (or reading!).
Also an attempt to open for writing
a pure-procedure program that is being executed.
.TP
27
\fBEFBIG\fR \- File too large
.br
The size of a file exceeded the maximum (about 16 mbytes).
.TP
*28
\fBENOSPC\fR \- No space left on device
.br
During a
.B write
to an ordinary file, there is no free space left on
the device.
.TP
29
\fBESPIPE\fR \- Illegal seek
.br
An
.B lseek
was issued to a pipe.
This error should also be issued
for other non-seekable devices.
.TP
30
\fBEROFS\fR \- Read-only file system
.br
An attempt to modify a file or directory was made on a device
mounted read-only.
.TP
31
\fBEMLINK\fR \- Too many links
.br
An attempt to make more than 127 links to a file.
.TP
32
\fBEPIPE\fR \- Broken pipe
.br
A write on a pipe for which there is no process to read the data.
This condition normally generates a signal; the error is returned
if the signal is ignored.
.TP
33
\fBEDOM\fR \- Math argument
.br
The argument of a function in the math package (3M) is out of
the domain of the function.
.TP
34
\fBERANGE\fR \- Result too large
.br
The value of a function in the math package (3M) is unrepresentable
within machine precision.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
intro(3)
.SH ASSEMBLER
Assembler interface is given for both PDP-11 and 8086 processors.
.TP
PDP-11:
The assembler symbols are defined in
.BR /usr/include/sys.s .
Return
values appear in registers R0 and R1; it is unwise to count on
these registers being preserved when no value is expected.
An
erroneous call is always indicated by turning on the C-bit of the
condition codes.
The error number is returned in R0.
The presence
of an error is most easily tested by the instructions
.B bes
and
.B bec
(`branch on error set (or clear)').
These are synonyms for
the
.B bcs
and
.B bcc
instructions.
.TP
8086:
Return values appear in registers AX, DX and CX; it is unwise
to count on these registers being preserved when no value is
expected.
An erroneous call is always indicated by an error
number in CX.
The presence of an error is most easily tested by
the instruction
.B JCXZ
(``jmp CX zero'').
.PP
.ce
